// en-t800 — English as spoken by the machine from the future.
//
// The accent is Austrian-German-influenced English, and only the two features
// that carry it are taken. Recognisability is not the same as thoroughness: a
// full transfer accent would be a costume, while these two are the ones anyone
// can hear and neither of them changes which WORD you heard.
//
//   w -> v    the single most identifiable feature of German-accented English
//   T -> z    /theta/ is not in the German inventory; z is the usual production
//   D -> d    and the voiced pair goes to /d/, which is the same substitution
//
// Deliberately NOT taken: final-obstruent devoicing. It is real and it is
// characteristic, and it turns "bad" into "bat" and "leave" into "leaf" — it
// changes the word, and a voice that cannot be trusted to say the word is no
// longer usable as a reading voice, however good the impression.

name en-t800
language en-t800 8
language en 22

phonemes en

// DELIBERATE. Longer syllables across the board, and a smaller gap between
// stressed and unstressed than English wants — the flatness of someone speaking
// a second language carefully, which happens to be the flatness of something
// that does not need to hurry.
stressLength 195 195 200 200 225 225 240 250
stressAmp 16 16 17 18 20 22 25 27

words 2
dictrules 8

replace 00 w v
replace 00 T z
replace 00 D d
